00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
 
a global crisis  needs global leadership for most of the  past 75 years  that leader has been america  no longer leaders held an online summit  today pledging billions to develop a  coronavirus vaccine  notably absent from the meeting were any  officials from the trump administration  i'm instructing my administration to  halt funding of the  world health organization america's  retreat has left a vacuum at the top  every time that america steps back  that's an opportunity for china to step  forward and present  itself as the champion of the world  order  has china spotted an opportunity to  steal a march on america  and use the soft power of global  diplomacy to hasten its rise  as the world's top power  there's one thing we must learn it's one  world  it's a world in which we must all get  along  and it's my opinion that this great  republican lead the way  as the second world war drew to an end  plans were put in place  to keep the peace the united nations and  other newly created institutions  performed the backbone of a rules-based  world order  and america was the chief architect and  leader  united nations have been working for  eight weeks to produce a charter on  which the future of world peace will  depend  since its creation in 1945 the  membership of the un  has grown from 51 countries to 193  its activities and those of its  specialized agencies  span almost every aspect of life  we have before us the opportunity to  forge  for ourselves and for future generations  a new world order  but after successive presidents  committed to leading and shaping the  world  president trump has pulled america in a  very different direction  the future does not belong to globalists  the future belongs to patriots the  future belongs to sovereign and  independent nations  under donald trump's presidency america  has been  a quitter of international arrangements  and institutions  as well as withdrawing funding for the  world health organization  a un agency president trump has pulled  america out of  the paris agreement on climate change  the nuclear deal with iran  and he abandoned the trans-pacific  partnership the giant trade agreement  that was close to completion  i think this is all part of um the  america first  idea there's a long history of america  fearing  being held down by by smaller powers but  it's taken a particularly strong form  under donald trump you know what i am  i'm a nationalist okay  america is so central to an order that  has  really stood the world in good stead for  75 years  if america withdraws everybody else is  extremely  worried about what will maintain this  order in its absence and whether the  rules of the game are actually going to  be adhered to in future the country that  has stepped into the void left by  america  is china china is becoming more and more  powerful  on the economic front technologically  and increasingly geopolitically as well  over the past two decades china's  contribution to the un's regular budget  has increased from 1 to 12  while america's has remained the same  china has been methodically increasing  its influence in existing institutions  not least the un chinese nationals now  had four of the un's 15  specialized agencies americans lead  just one china is has become in  recent years um increasingly assertive  in the way that it pushes  its interests it's not afraid anymore to  use quite strong-armed tactics it's not  afraid to use  its veto in the security council  [Music]  security council vetoes can tell us a  lot about the balance of power in the  u.n  during the cold war america used its  veto regularly  but in the past decade it's used it just  three times  china on the other hand has increased  its use  but the country exercising its veto  power most in recent years  is russia it does seem to be an alliance  of convenience between china  and russia both share an interest in  not having western countries become too  busy body in their human rights for  example  to be able to act uninhibitedly in their  rather authoritarian  ways whether over the long term that's  sustainable is debatable because russia  is after all a declining power  compared with china which is a rising  power and russia may come to  find its embrace of china distinctly  uncomfortable  in march 2018 france proposed that the  un security council  heard a briefing on human rights abuses  in syria  but russia and china opposed the session  the provisional agenda has not been  adopted it having failed to obtain a  required number of votes  the meeting is adjourned  china managed to prevent that by  lobbying very hard with  ivory coast former french colony which  switched sides at the last moment and  that's those sort of little incidents  are wake up calls for the west that  china's  influence is really starting to be felt  what's more  chinese officials have been inserting  favored chinese wording  into un documents phrases like win-win  cooperation  phrases that refer to the belt and road  phrases that take  a rather chinese view if you like of  of human rights cumulatively it can have  the effect of creating a chinese  friendly environment  for these multinational institutions  china has also been building channels of  influence  outside the institutions that america  designed  it has set up the asian infrastructure  investment bank  which encroaches on the world bank's  territory  and created the shanghai cooperation  organization  an eight-member group that includes  russia india  and pakistan it's not obvious that china  actually wants to use serp america's  role in the sense of taking on wider  responsibilities for the global order  but it certainly wants to make the world  safer for chinese  expansion this has worried those in the  west  not least because of china's attitude  towards human rights  if china does continue its rise in the  west retreat  then i think human rights would be in  trouble and that one of the criticisms  of the secretary general  is that he hasn't put human rights front  and center  and defended them as as  prominently as he might have done you  can watch antonio gutierrez  the un secretary general responding to  this and other questions the economist  put to him  by clicking on the link above  there has been some pushback from  america in january the state department  appointed a senior diplomat  mark lambert to counter the so-called  malign influence  of china and others at the u.n  but there is a sense that america is  perhaps no longer as dependable  as it once was you hear talk of  countries getting together  to try and keep this rules-based liberal  order  going um it's not easy to do this  without the real impetus that comes from  a power with the size and the weight  that america can put behind it  but smaller efforts at least can make  some difference  kovid 19 has shown that the biggest  problems cry out for international  efforts  climate change is a challenge that no  country can tackle alone  the same is true for organized crime and  cybercrime  a new president in the white house might  give america a renewed interest  in looking outward again but the un and  other global bodies  also need to reform their 75 year old  structures  are not designed for a 21st century  world  perhaps it will take something of a  crisis to make it happen  and to give countries the push they need  to modernize the institutions  the coronavirus pandemic has hijacked  the global agenda  it also presents an opportunity the  upheaval could spur countries into  strengthening the liberal world order  but if america fails to reassert its  global commitments  china's diplomatic power will continue  to grow  i've written a special report about the  united nations 75 years after its  founding  and how it's struggling to adjust to  changes in world power  especially the relationship between  america and china you can read it by  clicking the link  opposite thank you for watching  [Music]

